{
  "case_id": "2017-16-1362",
  "document_hash": "32d02cf33b4504b407cfc75a34f0d6923d8a6bb5b231dba9b10219756dc4f8cd",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Paul D. Clement)",
      "case_id": "2017-16-1362",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "32d02cf33b4504b407cfc75a34f0d6923d8a6bb5b231dba9b10219756dc4f8cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-32d02cf33b45-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Service advisors are salespeople primarily engaged in servicing automobiles. Service advisors are plainly salespeople, and what they sell and what they are primarily engaged in is the servicing of automobiles. Thus, Respondents and the nation's 100,000 service advisors come within the literal disjunctive text of the FLSA exemption for any salesman, partsman, or mechanic primarily engaged in selling or servicing automobiles, trucks, or farm implements."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2017-16-1362",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "32d02cf33b4504b407cfc75a34f0d6923d8a6bb5b231dba9b10219756dc4f8cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-32d02cf33b45-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Is -- is there any other category that -- the statute lists three categories: Salesmen, partsmen, and mechanics."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Paul D. Clement)",
      "case_id": "2017-16-1362",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "32d02cf33b4504b407cfc75a34f0d6923d8a6bb5b231dba9b10219756dc4f8cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-32d02cf33b45-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "There -- there is, Justice Ginsburg, if what you mean is sort of an occupation that was well understood at the time that Congress passed this exemption in 1966. The example would be automobile body repairmen. It was treated as a separate category, for example, in the Occupational Outlook Handbook that my friends on the other side like so much, but yet the -- the Labor Department has consistently treated repairmen in the body shop as covered by the exemption, just like mechanics in the service shop."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2017-16-1362",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "32d02cf33b4504b407cfc75a34f0d6923d8a6bb5b231dba9b10219756dc4f8cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-32d02cf33b45-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 18,
        "start": 18
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, why can't you say that this -- the partsmen, they're exempt because Congress put them there specifically and you don't have to match them with anything."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Clement",
      "case_id": "2017-16-1362",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "32d02cf33b4504b407cfc75a34f0d6923d8a6bb5b231dba9b10219756dc4f8cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-32d02cf33b45-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And that's all part of what they are primarily engaged in, which is not just facing the car. It's really -- their responsibility is the parts, whether it's the mechanic that wants the parts for a particular repair or whether it's some outside customer who wants to buy a part because they're a do-it-yourselfer."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2017-16-1362",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "32d02cf33b4504b407cfc75a34f0d6923d8a6bb5b231dba9b10219756dc4f8cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-32d02cf33b45-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: Is -- is everybody who's in a service department -- does everybody count as primarily engaged in servicing automobiles who's in a service department, the receptionist, the filers?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2017-16-1362",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "32d02cf33b4504b407cfc75a34f0d6923d8a6bb5b231dba9b10219756dc4f8cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-32d02cf33b45-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: What about an automobile upholsterer?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "James A. Feldman",
      "case_id": "2017-16-1362",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "32d02cf33b4504b407cfc75a34f0d6923d8a6bb5b231dba9b10219756dc4f8cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-32d02cf33b45-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 31,
        "start": 31
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The exemption in this case is for any salesman, partsman, or mechanic primarily engaged in selling or servicing automobiles. The most obvious reason why or the clearest reason why service advisors don't come within that exemption is they don't service automobiles."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "James A. Feldman",
      "case_id": "2017-16-1362",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "32d02cf33b4504b407cfc75a34f0d6923d8a6bb5b231dba9b10219756dc4f8cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-32d02cf33b45-0001-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "any court has ever construed it to mean anything other than what Black's Law Dictionary says, which is to do something customarily or regularly or continually."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2017-16-1362",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "32d02cf33b4504b407cfc75a34f0d6923d8a6bb5b231dba9b10219756dc4f8cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-32d02cf33b45-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: Mr. Feldman, when -\u00ad when you talked about the purposes, you focused on the fact that service advisors work on-site and don't work irregular hours."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2017-16-1362",
      "confidence": 0.94,
      "document_hash": "32d02cf33b4504b407cfc75a34f0d6923d8a6bb5b231dba9b10219756dc4f8cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-32d02cf33b45-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. FELDMAN: I -- I think that commissions are actually completely irrelevant to this provision. There's many other people at the car dealership, including some -- many of the ones who I mentioned who are paid on commissions, who are not exempt."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2017-16-1362",
      "confidence": 0.96,
      "document_hash": "32d02cf33b4504b407cfc75a34f0d6923d8a6bb5b231dba9b10219756dc4f8cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-32d02cf33b45-0002-0003",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 48,
        "start": 48
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In 2016, about two years ago, this Court remanded the case to the Ninth Circuit."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2017-16-1362",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "32d02cf33b4504b407cfc75a34f0d6923d8a6bb5b231dba9b10219756dc4f8cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-32d02cf33b45-0002-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 50,
        "start": 50
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Mr. -- Mr. Feldman, I -- I don't understand from what you said whether you are disagreeing with me that there would be no retroactive liability because employers relied in good faith on what had been the agency's position."
    },
